{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi3Q6fCGHnxCXYCkuzFy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Results:** Dq = 977 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 899 cm⁻¹, C ≈ 3131 cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 3.48). Energies are measured from the ³A₂g ground state. These values were worked out by hand; no tools were used.\n\n**1. Finding Dq from ³A₂ → ³T₂**\n\nFor octahedral d⁸, this transition energy equals 10Dq exactly.\n- 10Dq = 9 770 cm⁻¹, so **Dq = 977 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**2. Finding B from the two ³A₂ → ³T₁ bands**\n\nThe two ³T₁ energies are the roots of a 2×2 matrix:\n\nE(³T₁) = 15Dq + 7.5B ∓ ½√(225B² − 180·Dq·B + 100Dq²)\n\nThe square-root terms cancel when the two roots are added, so their sum is simple:\n- ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B\n- 16 050 + 26 740 = 42 790 = 29 310 + 15B\n- **B ≈ 898.7 cm⁻¹**\n\nCheck: putting Dq and B back into the formula gives 15 938 and 26 852 cm⁻¹, against the observed 16 050 and 26 740 cm⁻¹. The fit is consistent to about 110 cm⁻¹. Using the sum gives B a value that sits between the two bands rather than matching either one exactly.\n\n**3. Finding C from ¹E**\n\n¹E comes from two strong-field configurations, t₂⁶e² and t₂⁴e⁴, which mix. I used the d² ¹E matrix as the hole equivalent, with the sign of Dq reversed. I took the matrix elements from memory of the standard d² tables (Griffith) rather than looking them up.\n\nRelative to ³A₂, the matrix is:\n- t₂⁶e² diagonal: 8B + 2C\n- t₂⁴e⁴ diagonal: 20Dq + 9B + 2C\n- off-diagonal: −2√3·B\n\nThe lower root is:\n\nE(¹E) = 2C + (20Dq + 17B)/2 − √[((20Dq + B)/2)² + 12B²]\n\nSubstituting Dq and B:\n- (20Dq + 17B)/2 = 17 408.9\n- √(10 219.4² + 9.69×10⁶) = 10 683.1\n- So E(¹E) = 2C + 6 725.8\n\nSetting this equal to the observed 12 987 cm⁻¹ gives 2C = 6 261, so **C ≈ 3 131 cm⁻¹**.\n\n{\"Dq\": 977, \"B\": 899, \"C\": 3131, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from the 3T2 band, B from the sum of the two 3T1 bands, C from the 2x2 1E matrix.\"}\n\n<br>"}],"container":null,"stop_reason